{
 "id": "SHW-18",
 "name": "dna-helix",
 "version": 1,
 "title": "The double helix: a 3D DNA model that unzips",
 "summary": "Twenty base pairs of B-DNA in 3D: two sugar-phosphate backbones of beads on a string and base-pair rungs coloured by base (adenine coral, thymine amber, guanine blue, cytosine teal), with the real twist (10 pairs a turn) and minor groove. It turns slowly, a drag turns and tilts it, and an Unzip button eases the two strands apart from the top down, each half-rung staying with its strand; a caption gives the sequence and a legend. Ten meshes from expressions, no per-part geometry.",
 "prompt": "Show a DNA double helix in 3D: two backbones of spheres and base-pair rungs coloured by base (A-T, G-C), turning slowly, draggable, with a caption; add an unzip animation. A crisp scientific-illustration look in light and dark.",
 "level": 5,
 "family": "SHW",
 "hand_built": "The whole molecule is ten meshExpression3D surfaces (two beaded tubes along the helices and eight sets of half-rungs, one per strand and base, whose pairs are read from 20-bit masks inside the expression), which neither Scene3D nor Chart3D can express.",
 "capabilities": {
